+## Teammate Communication
+
+Communication is **off by default**. Enable it for one parallel `subagent` call with `communication: true`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "communication": true,
+  "tasks": [
+    { "agent": "worker", "task": "Investigate the interface constraints. Share findings with worker-2." },
+    { "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement the interface. Incorporate findings from worker-1." },
+    { "agent": "reviewer", "task": "Review the interface and broadcast rejected approaches." }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+The usual agent scope and project trust checks still apply. Communication is rejected in single and chain modes. Workers remain separate Pi processes, with their configured model, thinking level, working directory, system prompt, and tools. Enabled workers additionally receive the two tools below, even when the agent definition restricts its tools. There is no additional parent tool or UI.
+
+### Addresses and tools
+
+Workers are named `worker-1`, `worker-2`, etc., in `tasks` array order. Addresses identify **task instances**, not agent definitions: the two `worker` tasks above have distinct addresses. Workers receive their identity and an initial roster in their system prompt. Parallel result headings and details also include the worker ID.
+
+`team_members({})` returns the current roster, including self:
+
+```json
+{
+  "kind": "roster",
+  "self": "worker-1",
+  "workers": [
+    { "id": "worker-1", "agent": "worker", "task": "Investigate...", "state": "running" },
+    { "id": "worker-2", "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement...", "state": "running" },
+    { "id": "worker-3", "agent": "reviewer", "task": "Review...", "state": "finished" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+States are `pending` (queued or still starting), `running` (ready to accept findings), and `finished` (no longer accepting findings). The existing limit remains eight tasks, four concurrent; queued workers cannot receive messages.
+
+Private message, visible only to the recipient:
+
+```json
+{ "to": "worker-2", "text": "The interface requires an AbortSignal. The synchronous adapter cannot satisfy it." }
+```
+
+Pass these arguments to `team_send`. For a broadcast, use `"to": "*"`:
+
+```json
+{ "to": "*", "text": "Rejected the shared singleton approach: it leaks state between dispatches." }
+```
+
+The result lists acceptance and failures separately. For example, when one teammate has finished:
+
+```json
+{
+  "kind": "sent",
+  "accepted": ["worker-2"],
+  "failed": [{ "id": "worker-3", "reason": "Worker is finished" }]
+}
+```
+
+Broadcasts exclude the sender. Pending, finished, and full-inbox teammates appear in `failed`; other running teammates still receive the broadcast. Inspect both arrays. Do not retry successful recipients merely because others failed. Self sends fail, and unknown addresses produce an explicit error. Sending never waits for a model reply, and never starts or restarts a worker.
+
+### Delivery and limits
+
+A successful send means the parent broker has **accepted** the full finding for delivery, not that the recipient has read or agreed with it. The broker assigns the sender identity from the sender's process channel; a tool argument cannot impersonate another worker.
+
+After each model response and all its tools finish, the worker extension automatically takes its accepted findings and queues them as one context message. That batch preserves every sender's order, includes each sender's worker ID and agent name, and retains complete text. It appears in the next model call, including all findings accepted before that turn finished. The model does not poll an inbox, and delivery does not interrupt or skip running tools. Batching also works with Pi's default one-at-a-time steering setting.
+
+If a worker would otherwise finish, taking pending findings keeps it running for another model call. Checking an empty final inbox and marking the worker finished is one broker operation: a concurrent send either gets accepted and processed, or fails because the worker has finished. There is no interval where a successful send can be silently lost to normal shutdown. Findings are delivered once during normal execution.
+
+Messages must contain non-whitespace text and be at most **65,536 UTF-8 bytes**. Unicode, line breaks, and supplementary characters are preserved; oversized text and malformed Unicode are rejected, never truncated. Each recipient's pending inbox is limited to **1,048,576 UTF-8 text bytes or 256 messages**, whichever comes first. A full inbox causes a per-recipient failure, not silent dropping. Invalid tool arguments produce explicit errors.
+
+### Isolation and lifecycle
+
+Transport uses inherited Node IPC channels, one per worker, with a new in-memory broker per dispatch. No listening endpoints, mailbox files, environment credentials, persistent history, or shared global team are created. Reusing worker IDs in concurrent or later dispatches cannot connect their messages. The parent extension must run under a runtime supporting Node child-process IPC; source TypeScript invocation is supported without a build.
+
+Cancelling the parent stops active children, prevents queued children from starting, discards the cancelled team's queues, and closes its channels. Normal completion releases the same resources. Cancellation, process crashes, and unrecoverable model failures are not delivery guarantees; there is no crash recovery. Cross-session communication, nested teams, persistent shared memory, and coordination after a teammate finishes are not supported. This is dispatch isolation, not a security sandbox against arbitrary code running as the same OS user.
+
+When `communication` is omitted or false, no IPC channel or worker messaging extension is added, and existing subagent configuration and behavior remain unchanged.
+
